\chapter{System Overview and Module Decomposition}
\label{chap:system-overview}

\section{Introduction}
The sovereign physics engine designed by C.~Matthew Blackwell integrates
classical gravitation, relativistic curvature, negative mass dynamics, and
topological metric manipulation into a unified computational architecture.
This chapter provides a high-level overview of the system, describing each
major subsystem, its responsibilities, and its interactions with the rest of
the engine.

The architecture is modular, parallelized, and designed for high-performance
execution on heterogeneous compute platforms including CPUs, GPUs, and
distributed clusters.

\section{Global Architecture}
The engine is organized into five primary subsystems:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Particle Dynamics Core}
    \item \textbf{Metric Tensor Solver}
    \item \textbf{Topology and Wormhole Module}
    \item \textbf{Anomaly Detection and Stability Layer}
    \item \textbf{Telemetry, Logging, and Encryption Layer}
\end{enumerate}

Each subsystem operates independently but communicates through shared memory
buffers and synchronized compute pipelines.

\section{Particle Dynamics Core}
The Particle Dynamics Core is responsible for:
\begin{itemize}
    \item resolving Bondi--Hermann force interactions,
    \item computing inertial acceleration responses,
    \item updating particle positions and velocities,
    \item maintaining reciprocal mass caches,
    \item preventing division-by-zero anomalies.
\end{itemize}

The core update loop is:
\begin{equation}
    \vb{a}_i = \sum_{j \neq i}
    \frac{-G m_{gi} m_{gj}}{r_{ij}^2}\,\hat{\vb{r}}_{ij}
    \cdot \frac{1}{m_{ii}},
\end{equation}
executed in parallel across all particles.

\section{Metric Tensor Solver}
The Metric Tensor Solver computes spacetime curvature using a discretized
form of the Einstein field equations:
\begin{equation}
    G_{\mu\nu} = 8\pi G\, T_{\mu\nu}.
\end{equation}

Responsibilities include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item evaluating Alcubierre-class warp metrics,
    \item computing throat stability conditions,
    \item updating curvature fields,
    \item enforcing energy condition violations for exotic matter.
\end{itemize}

The solver operates on a 3+1 decomposition grid, updating:
\begin{equation}
    g_{\mu\nu}(t + \Delta t)
\end{equation}
each simulation tick.

\section{Topology and Wormhole Module}
This subsystem manages:
\begin{itemize}
    \item wormhole throat radius sweeps,
    \item topology corruption detection,
    \item encrypted coordinate key generation,
    \item metric continuity verification,
    \item dynamic topology locking.
\end{itemize}

The throat stability condition is:
\begin{equation}
    \rho(r_0) \le \rho_{\text{crit}},
\end{equation}
with collapse triggered when the inequality is violated.

\section{Anomaly Detection and Stability Layer}
This layer monitors:
\begin{itemize}
    \item runaway acceleration loops,
    \item negative mass clustering,
    \item metric discontinuities,
    \item topology tearing events,
    \item divergence in reciprocal mass caches.
\end{itemize}

Anomalies are classified into:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Type I: benign numerical artifacts,
    \item Type II: unstable physical configurations,
    \item Type III: catastrophic topology failures.
\end{itemize}

\section{Telemetry, Logging, and Encryption Layer}
This subsystem provides:
\begin{itemize}
    \item real-time simulation telemetry,
    \item encrypted logging of metric states,
    \item topology handshake verification every 1024 ticks,
    \item secure archival of wormhole configurations,
    \item compression of curvature fields for long-term storage.
\end{itemize}

All logs are encrypted using coordinate-key hashing to prevent unauthorized
metric reconstruction.

